  "summary": "An induced draft (ID) fan sits after the boiler, dust collector or scrubber and sucks the hot flue gas out of the furnace, keeping it slightly below atmospheric pressure (balanced draft). It handles hot, dusty and sometimes corrosive gas, so it is robust, heavily built and often controlled by inlet dampers, inlet vanes or a variable-speed drive.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Fan (dynamic) — boiler draft fan",
    "type": "Centrifugal, inlet box, louvre damper control, coupled motor"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "gas": "Flue gas ~120–200 °C after air heater (plant specific)",
    "control": "Inlet louvre damper (VFD common on new plants)"
  },
